    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Daulton Varsho** (69.94) and **Taylor Ward** (67.66) anchor our outfield, standing as the highest-projected starters due to favorable park factors and advantageous platoon splits against Quantrill and Griffin, respectively. **Daulton Varsho** benefits significantly from a high-efficiency matchup against a suspect arm, while **Taylor Ward** leverages a massive +16% park adjustment to offset his moderate difficulty against Griffin. Additionally, **Luis Arraez** (66.05) is locked in at the top of the order; his elite contact profile combined with a strong wind-out projection at his home park makes him our most reliable source of early-inning efficiency. \n\nThe most difficult roster decision landed on the bench, where **Geraldo Perdomo** (47.91) narrowly missed the final UTIL spot. While **Geraldo Perdomo** carries the benefit of a premium order position (#2), his projected efficiency was suppressed by a poor park factor and a tough matchup against Sulser, ultimately ceding the spot to the lower-scoring but safer-floor **Jeremiah Jackson** (48.60). We remain cautious regarding **Drew Gilbert** (51.56), whose late-order placement (#8) creates significant downside risk despite his strong platoon edge against Elder. \n\nManagers should keep a close eye on the weather and potential late-scratches, particularly given that our bench depth\u2014including **Gavin Sheets** and **Lawrence Butler**\u2014is currently constrained by unfavorable matchups against elite arms like Yamamoto and Detmers. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains sidelined on the IL, leaving a noticeable void in our heart-of-the-order production. We are relying on the speed-heavy profiles of **Masyn Winn**, **Jung Hoo Lee**, and **Matt Chapman** to capitalize on the \"Slow SP Delivery\" factor today, as stealing bags against these specific opposing batteries is a key pillar of our daily optimization strategy.",
